2
00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:27.945
What I'm going to talk
about is like what happens

3
00:00:27.945 --> 00:00:31.070
after it gets diffused and
adopted inside companies,

4
00:00:31.070 --> 00:00:34.195
like how do roles change,
how does expertise change,

5
00:00:34.195 --> 00:00:37.320
what happens to the
organizational structure aspect.

6
00:00:37.413 --> 00:00:41.434
So the idea of a bundle is: if you
think of a job as a bundle of tasks,

7
00:00:41.434 --> 00:00:44.606
there is a glue that holds
the different tasks together.

8
00:00:44.606 --> 00:00:47.664
Say if you're a coder,
there are certain tasks you do.

9
00:00:47.664 --> 00:00:50.836
If you're an M&A lawyer,
there are certain tasks you do.

10
00:00:50.836 --> 00:00:53.441
If you're a radiologist,
you do certain tasks.

11
00:00:53.441 --> 00:00:55.933
Some of those jobs,
the glue is very strong.

12
00:00:56.063 --> 00:00:58.985
So if you try to unbundle a
job that has a strong glue,

13
00:00:58.985 --> 00:01:03.289
we have found time and again through
empirical research that it's not going well.

14
00:01:03.289 --> 00:01:05.733
The performance of that
person suffers, right?

15
00:01:05.767 --> 00:01:08.872
…that was used, this
particular firm called LegalCo,

16
00:01:08.872 --> 00:01:11.201
it's a pseudonym, it's in the Bay Area.

17
00:01:11.201 --> 00:01:13.889
They adopted this tool
like way back in 2022,

18
00:01:13.889 --> 00:01:16.935
even before ChatGPT 3.5
was released to the public.

19
00:01:16.935 --> 00:01:18.547
They were an early adopter.

20
00:01:18.567 --> 00:01:22.118
Essentially, it allows people
to create these long contracts,

21
00:01:22.118 --> 00:01:24.913
100-page contracts,
faster by combining clauses.

22
00:01:24.913 --> 00:01:27.999
And what we found was when
we were studying the firm,

23
00:01:27.999 --> 00:01:30.677
analyzing the logs,
like doing a lot of other…

24
00:01:30.700 --> 00:01:32.751
But I'm worried, like, in the future,

25
00:01:32.751 --> 00:01:35.467
how would management deploy
this tool in a manner

26
00:01:35.467 --> 00:01:37.906
that my own role would
become weaker, right?

27
00:01:37.906 --> 00:01:41.010
It would be consolidated into
a shared service of sorts.

28
00:01:41.033 --> 00:01:43.938
But even more importantly,
what more you could do?

29
00:01:43.938 --> 00:01:47.598
What more complex, higher-order
tasks potentially you could do,

30
00:01:47.598 --> 00:01:49.458
given the opening created by AI,

31
00:01:49.458 --> 00:01:51.433
given the excess capacity created?

32
00:01:51.633 --> 00:01:53.278
…not for any strategic reasons.

33
00:01:53.278 --> 00:01:55.824
This person thought perhaps
this is the best way

34
00:01:55.824 --> 00:01:58.371
to position this technology
when introducing it.

35
00:01:58.371 --> 00:02:01.713
And we call it, as researchers,
we call it like job enrichment.

36
00:02:01.733 --> 00:02:05.371
Obviously you're going to
see issues on AI slop, right?

37
00:02:05.371 --> 00:02:09.737
If you're using the old throughput
metrics, in the paralegal case,

38
00:02:09.737 --> 00:02:13.573
if the divisions are still
using metrics such as caseload.

39
00:02:13.700 --> 00:02:15.270
That did not change, right?

40
00:02:15.270 --> 00:02:17.944
These divisions just
increased their workload,

41
00:02:17.944 --> 00:02:21.375
but did not like really match
how they're getting evaluated

42
00:02:21.375 --> 00:02:23.410
accordingly at the end of the year.
